From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:18:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: stay down after host sleep link loss

When the PCIe link does not come back after system sleep, the host
bridge marks the device disconnected. brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3() then went
on to access the BARs, which can stall the CPU. Return -ENODEV and keep
the bus down instead; firmware commands and transmits are then refused
early.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c    | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
index d7c6a402f..a07f7c8ad 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -2696,6 +2696,16 @@ static int brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3(struct device *dev)
 	devinfo = bus->bus_priv.pcie->devinfo;
 	brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Enter, dev=%p, bus=%p\n", dev, bus);
 
+	/*
+	 * The host bridge cut the device off because its link did not come
+	 * back. Any BAR access now could stall the CPU, so stay down; with the
+	 * bus down, firmware commands and transmits are refused early.
+	 */
+	if (pci_dev_is_disconnected(devinfo->pdev)) {
+		brcmf_err(bus, "device lost over suspend, staying down\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
 	/* Check if device is still up and running, if so we are ready */
 	if (brcmf_pcie_read_reg32(devinfo, devinfo->reginfo->intmask) != 0) {
 		brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Try to wakeup device....\n");
